{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"reasoning","text":"431,396 reads, mostly 150–151 bp (the 151bp ones are probably 197k + 73k at 150… actually, the 151 count is 197,108, the 150 count is 73,558 — hmm, if you include the newline… no wait, I stripped it. Well, whatever, mostly 150/151 bp). This is an exome capture of chr9, single-end 150bp. About 431k reads × 150bp ≈ 64.7 Mb — that's roughly 0.5x of chr9 (chr9 is about 138 Mb)? Wait no, it's an \"exome\" — chr9's exome is about 1.5% of 138 Mb ≈ 2 Mb, so 64.7 Mb is about 30x coverage of the exome. Makes sense.\n\nOK, alignment approach: minimap2 -x sr is available in WSL. And we need samtools for sorting/pileup. Options:\n1. Install samtools via apt in WSL (modifies the WSL distro — outside the workspace).\n2. Install samtools+bwa via conda into a workspace-local prefix (writes to the workspace, but conda's package cache goes outside unless redirected — can set CONDA_PKGS_DIRS).\n3. Write pure-Python alignment… too risky.\n\nActually, hmm, could we avoid samtools entirely? minimap2 outputs SAM. Parsing SAM in Python is fine. Pileup in pure Python is also feasible: parse the CIGAR strings and accumulate base counts per position. 431k reads is totally manageable in Python (might take a few minutes). But quality-aware, indel-aware pileup needs care. If the variants are SNVs (nonsense SNVs), handling CIGARs with I/D/N clips should be enough.\n\nBut actually, installing samtools via conda into a workspace prefix is cleaner and gives us mpileup for a high-confidence call. Let me check whether conda works and whether we have internet access. Per the environment description, network access is enabled.\n\nLet me try: conda create --prefix ./tools/env -c conda-forge -c bioconda samtools bwa — but the channels might need to be downloaded. Or just samtools alone (htslib is bundled). If we use minimap2, bwa is strictly not needed. But bwa-mem is the classic choice for exomes. minimap2 -x sr is also sufficient for variant calling.\n\nLet me set CONDA_PKGS_DIRS inside the workspace, create a prefix inside the workspace, and try conda.\n\nActually wait — I should also consider mamba/micromamba. Only conda.bat exists. OK.\n\nLet me try. If conda is slow/fails, fall back to pure-Python pileup on minimap2's SAM output.\n\nActually, let me also consider: the filename \"q2\" might mean the FASTQ has quality degradation… the quality strings look fine (mostly high quality). The \"q2\" in the filename is probably a benchmark replicate/quality tier.\n\nPlan:\n1. Unzip the reference genome to workspace/work/ (or leave it gzipped? minimap2 requires uncompressed or bgzipped fasta. It can read .gz but indexing requires decompression; minimap2 can read gzipped FASTA directly). Unzip to work/ inside the workspace.\n2. Align: minimap2 -ax sr ref.fa reads.fq.gz > aln.sam (in WSL, paths are /mnt/c/…). Actually, if we read from and write to /mnt/c, it'll be slower but fine (54MB of input). Or copy the files to WSL's tmp… no, that's outside the workspace. Use /mnt/c paths directly; performance: 64MB of alignment should be fast.\n3. Sort SAM -> BAM. If we can install samtools, use samtools sort. If not, pure-Python sort (431k reads is OK, but BAM writing needs bgzf… we can write SAM and use pysam if it's available — no, we don't have pysam). Let me try installing samtools via conda first.\n\nLet me try conda install into a workspace prefix."}}
