{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"reasoning","text":"Two nonsense candidates:\n\n1. chr9:122477222 G>T AF=0.462 depth=39, gene OR1J1 (olfactory receptor — olfactory receptors are not \"extremely LoF-intolerant\"; in fact they are LoF-tolerant; also AF 0.462 is close to heterozygous germline, and doesn't look like typical mosaic… although it could be mosaic too; but OR1J1 is an olfactory receptor gene and is very LoF-tolerant).\n2. chr9:127661125 G>T AF=0.194 depth=93, gene STXBP1 — STXBP1 is highly LoF-intolerant (pLI≈1, LOEUF≈0.06–0.15), and causes developmental epileptic encephalopathy. AF=0.194 → clear mosaic. Depth 93 is also good.\n\nSTXBP1 is the obvious answer: a highly LoF-intolerant gene, mosaic AF ~0.19, nonsense variant.\n\nHowever: \"GTF frame sanity mismatch: 14749\" — this is a concern. Frame mismatches could indicate a genome/annotation coordinate mismatch (e.g., chr9 in the annotation GTF is on GRCh38, but our reference is a different version?), or there could be a bug in my cds_coord/frame comparison. Let's investigate:\n\nThe GTF frame column indicates the number of bases to skip at the start of a CDS feature (reading frame). My check: for each CDS segment (in transcript order), compute the CDS coordinate of the segment's first base (5'-most base of that segment) and verify cc%3 == frame.\n\nHmm, wait — there's a subtle point here. The frame of a CDS feature gives the reading frame of the first base of that feature in transcript orientation. My cc is computed in transcript orientation, so for the first base of the segment (in transcript orientation), cc%3 should equal frame. This should hold. 14749 mismatches out of a total of ~30k CDS segments? Let's see how many segments there are. If it's partial, it might be due to transcripts with CDS length %3 != 0 (already excluded later, but the frame check runs on all transcripts), or due to selenoproteins / non-standard cases, or due to a bug: for '-' strand segments, \"the first base in transcript orientation\" is `end`, and I used gpos = start if '+' else end — correct. Then cds_coord_of(tx, gpos): binary search in ascending order, find the segment containing gpos, then compute. Seems correct.\n\nPossible causes of the mismatch: some transcripts have annotated CDS that don't start from complete codons, or there's a frame annotation problem with segment boundaries due to my cum calculation… Let's debug: count the number of mismatches and print some examples with gene names. Also count total number of segments.\n\nActually wait — one important bug candidate: in build_transcript, for '-' strand I take the reverse complement per segment: s = \"\".join(COMP[c] for c in reversed(s)) — correct.\n\ncds_coord_of: for strand '-', cc = cum[j] + (end - pos). For the first base in transcript orientation pos=end → cc = cum[j] + 0. Good.\n\nFrame check: for idx,(start,end,frame) in enumerate(segs_tx) — segs_tx is sorted by start in descending order for '-' — good, transcript order.\n\nHmm, so why 14749 mismatches? Let me look at an example. Maybe my parse of the frame column is wrong: cols[7] is the frame for CDS; no problem there.\n\nOr: does the GTF have \"CDS\" entries with a non-canonical order for some transcripts? Or, the frame mismatch is because those transcripts have CDS lengths not divisible by 3 → phase drifts in the downstream segments. Let me count mismatches broken down by whether the CDS length %3==0.\n\nLet's debug quickly. Also, I'll verify the OR1J1 variant — it's likely a mapping artifact due to the olfactory receptor gene family (paralogs). STXBP1 has depth 93, AF 0.194, G>T. Let's get the transcript-level annotation details (protein position, amino acid change, transcript ID) for both, and verify the STXBP1 variant with a manual check (look at the read support: look at reads covering the position, count by base/strand, verify in the original FASTQ… also verify the codon change: e.g., a codon like CAG (Gln) → TAG stop by G>T at position 1 of the codon, or GAG (Glu) → TAG? No, GAG G>T at position 1 → TAG, yes; or GGA (Gly) → TGA stop at position 1, etc.)\n\nAlso let me check which STXBP1 transcript: MANE Select ENST00000374266? Something like that. And the amino acid change.\n\nFurthermore, I need to re-verify the pileup at the position with independent logic (e.g., simple counting from SAM with different parsing) — cross-check.\n\nNext, LoF-intolerance evidence for STXBP1: gnomAD v4 LOEUF is about 0.10? pLI from ExAC/gnomAD v2.2 = 1.0? Let's verify with a web search (public knowledge, permitted: not the task's oracle). STXBP1 is a well-known haploinsufficient gene (OMIM 602926; developmental epileptic encephalopathy 4, AD). In gnomAD v4 constraint, STXBP1 LOEUF ≈ 0.08? pLI 1.0. Let's confirm with a search.\n\nAlso, regarding OR1J1 chr9:122477222: OR genes are extremely LoF-tolerant (LOEUF ~1). And AF 0.462 ≈ heterozygous — probably a germline variant or a mis-mapping artifact. The task asks for mosaic (typically AF < 0.5) and high-confidence + LoF-intolerant gene → STXBP1 wins on all criteria.\n\nPlan:\n1. Debug the frame mismatch (verify annotation consistency).\n2. Extract full annotation details for the STXBP1 variant; independent recount of supporting reads; verify codon/amino acid change; identify transcript (prefer MANE Select/basic).\n3. Verify constraint for STXBP1 on the web (gnomAD LOEUF/pLI).\n4. Write out outputs: variant.tsv, evidence.json, report.md. Extend analysis.py so that it deterministically selects the top variant (scoring) and writes out all three output files, so that everything can be regenerated by re-running the script.\n\nFirst, let me check the details of the two nonsense candidates in candidates.json.\n"}}
