Wait — another possibility: these transcripts may be on a fix PATCH sequence, and the GTF records for the patch have coordinates remapped to the primary chromosome with an offset. Hmm, but then start/end wouldn't match my reference at all.\n\nLet me test directly: extract the reference sequence of WASHC1's CDS, translate it, and see if I get a proper protein. If translation is normal (starts with M, ends with *, no internal stops), then my cum/phase is correct and the GTF frame field for those segments is simply inconsistent (or follows a convention I'm not modeling, e.g., frame of a minus-strand feature counted from the other end... but I showed that doesn't give 1 either).\n\nHmm wait, let me redo the \"other end\" calculation more carefully. For minus-strand segment 18381-18492, \"genomic 5'\" is 18381. Phase at 18381 in transcript orientation: cc = 149 + (18492-18381) = 260 → 260%3 = 2. Not 1.\n\nWhat if the frame field used (end-start+1) of this segment subtracted by 1 from the cum before this segment, i.e., frame = (cum - 1)%3 — equivalent to saying there's a 1bp intron retention in the CDS annotation...\n\nOK, alternative hypothesis — my parse picked up the wrong column because some GTF lines have a different column structure? No, GTF is tab-delimited, 9 columns.\n\nLet me verify with a definitive independent source: fetch the WASHC1 ENST00000442898.5 CDS from Ensembl and compare the phase. Or more simply: check the raw GTF lines for this transcript.\n\nActually — wait. I just remembered! GTF's \"frame\" for CDS is the phase at the start of the CDS feature, and in Ensembl GTF, the frame of a minus-strand CDS feature is... the Ensembl GTF FAQ says: \"phase... for features on the minus strand, phase is relative to the 5' end of the feature, i.e., the highest genomic coordinate.\" Hmm, I thought I remembered it this way. But the numbers contradict this.\n\nLet me test yet another convention: frame relative to the 3' end of the whole CDS region (translation stop 