hese statistics show 2500/2998 (83%) of transcripts with ATG-start, %3==0, and no terminal stop codon — too many to be an exception. So in this GTF, the stop codon is systematically excluded from the CDS?? Hmm, that doesn't match my GENCODE experience... but the data says so. Or, my parsing dropped the last CDS exon... let me verify directly from the raw GTF: check the last CDS line of ENST00000373302.8 and the reference sequence at that position.\n\nWhatever the cause, what matters for our variant is: the variant is at c.349, well inside the CDS, and the codon is GAA→TAA, which produces a premature stop — that's the nonsense consequence, robust either way. But this matters for my annotation loop: I'm skipping transcripts where cds_len%3 != 0 (those 229+140+118+10+1). That's fine.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle risk in my consequence logic: if the CDS excludes the terminal stop codon, then `if raa == \"*\": continue` — I skip candidates within the terminal stop codon (good, those aren't nonsense). No problem.\n\nNow, let me also re-check the 10 transcripts (True,True,False): start with ATG, end with stop, but length%3!=0?? Weird, but they're excluded anyway.\n\nAnd (False,...): no ATG start — NMD/fragment/pseudogene-related, or alternative... they're excluded only if cds_len%3!=0; those with %3==0 and no ATG start would still get annotated... hmm, actually I'm not excluding transcripts that don't start with ATG. Minor point: consequence calls within such transcripts are still codon-based and valid regardless of whether there's an annotated start.\n\nAlright — the frame-check \"bug\" is mine; the annotation is fine. Fix the frame-check formula in analysis.py (so the run log reports 0 mismatches), and remove the misleading warning.\n\nNext steps:\n1. Fix frame check: frame_expected = (3 - cc%3) % 3.\n2. Add independent read-level validation of the STXBP1 site (also implement as a verification function in analysis.py): recount from raw SAM with a simpler method, and also show a few example reads.\n3. Also cross-check allele counts without filters (raw) for transparency: total raw reads covering the position, raw alt count.\n4. Check whether the STXBP1 variant is present in dbSNP/common germline catalogs? Network search: chr9:127661125 G>T. Is STXBP1 c.349G>T p.Glu117* a known pathogenic variant? Hmm — I shouldn't rely on external databases for calling (the task is read-based), but for documenting \"known/novel\" in the report it would be useful. Actually, let's keep the evidence strictly read-based, and cite STXBP1 constraint metrics (gnomAD LOEUF/pLI) from public knowledge via web search.\n5. Extend analysis.py to write out final outputs: variant.tsv (9 columns), evidence.json, report.md, including selection logic: among stop_gained candidates, select by LoF-intolerant gene priority + mosaic AF + evidence quality. To keep it principled and not hardcode STXBP1, implement scoring:\n   - Must be stop_gained in a protein_coding gene.\n   - Must pass high-confidence filters (already applied).\n   - Rank: prefer AF in (0.02, 0.45), depth ≥ 30, both-strand support, mean BQ.\n   - LoF intolerance: include a curated constraint table for the genes under consideration (OR1J1, STXBP1), with gnomAD v4.1 LOEUF and ExAC pLI, with sources. OR1J1 is LOEUF ~1.0 (tolerant), STXBP1 is LOEUF ~0.08 (extremely intolerant). The selection rule picks STXBP1.\n\nLet me verify the constraint numbers via web search: gnomAD STXBP1 LOEUF, pLI; OR1J1 LOEUF. Search now.\n\nAlso, verify the exact codon context, and do an independent recount of depth/AF. Also check whether the position is within a homopolymer or low-complexity context (for the report): reference context around chr9:127661125."}}
