{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"reasoning","text":"Excellent — definitive evidence:\n\n- **STXBP1** (ENSG00000136854), gnomAD (v4, GRCh38): obs_lof=3 vs exp_lof=78.5, oe_lof=0.038, **LOEUF (oe_lof_upper)=0.0987**, lof_z=7.23, **pLI=1.0**, oe_lof_percentile=0 (top percentile of LoF-constrained genes). Extremely LoF-intolerant.\n- **OR1J1** (ENSG00000136834): no constraint data (flag \"no_exp_lof\") — typical for olfactory receptor genes, LoF-tolerant / excluded from constraint analysis.\n\nTherefore the answer is chr9:127661125 G>T in STXBP1, c.349G>T p.Glu117* (in the canonical transcript ENST00000373302.8; c.307/p.Glu103* in shorter 5' variants), AF=0.194.\n\nNext, do independent read-level validation of this position, then finalize analysis.py and produce the final output. Independent verification: extract all reads overlapping position 127661125 from the SAM, count bases in both directions with simple logic, and also check MAPQ distribution, read position of the alt base, presence of the alt in the original FASTQ. Also check the reference context (±10 bp) and confirm it's a G>T SNV in a non-repetitive context.\n\nAdditionally, verify that this position doesn't match a common dbSNP germline variant (mosaic claim): I could query the dbSNP API for chr9:127661125… but that's an external database; the task calls for identifying mosaic variants from the reads. Given the AF of 0.194, it's clearly mosaic (not 0.5/1.0). Optionally, I could mention that no population match was checked. Keep it read-based; optionally, check NCBI for known variants at that position for the report. Let me try dbSNP E-utilities quickly — if it works, fine; if not, skip. Actually, NCBI SPDI: https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/variation/v0/spdi/chr9:127661125:G:T/seq_id… hmm, that's the variation services API: https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/variation/v0/… For now let me try dbSNP's search endpoint. It might not be essential — evidence of mosaicism comes from the AF itself. I'll try one lightweight query; if it's slow or fails, move on.\n\nAlso, re-confirm that the OR1J1 variant is AF 0.46 (near-heterozygous germline) — not consistent with the \"mosaic\" framing, plus OR genes are mapping-prone. Good grounds to exclude it in the report.\n\nIndependent recount plan (write to work/verify.py):\n- Walk the SAM, for reads overlapping 127661125 with MAPQ>=0 (no filter, count everything): compute base at the position (via CIGAR walk), baseQ, strand, read name.\n- Report: total count of overlapping reads (all primary), counts by base, counts after mapq>=20 & bq>=20 filter (should match 93/18), alt fwd/rev, alt read-position distribution, alt baseQ distribution, MAPQ of alt reads.\n- Also for OR1J1 122477222 similarly, for comparison.\n\nNext, finalize analysis.py: fix the frame-check formula; add selection + output-writer sections that produce variant.tsv, evidence.json, report.md. Make selection rule-based:\n1. stop_gained in protein_coding genes.\n2. Mosaic AF 0.02–0.45 (OR1J1's 0.462 already falls outside MAX_AF=0.50, but is inside the (0.02,0.50) window… hmm, 0.462 < 0.50, so it passes. To separate mosaic from germline het, I could tighten MAX_AF to 0.45 in the \"mosaic selection\" step (documented). But careful: real mosaic variants can occasionally reach AF ~0.45–0.5 (early embryonic). For the selection (not calling), I'll use the gene constraint + quality rank as the primary discriminant, plus require AF ≤ 0.45 for a mosaic designation, and note OR1J1 as an excluded candidate (likely germline het, OR gene family, no constraint evidence).\n\nGene constraint data: embed a small JSON table (work/constraint_raw.json) in evidence.json, document the source (gnomAD API GRCh38, accessed 2026-08-27). analysis.py will load work/constraint_raw.json if it exists and use LOEUF/pLI in selection (prefer genes with oe_lof_upper < 0.35 and pLI > 0.9, i.e., the standard \"highly LoF-intolerant\" definition). If the file is absent, fall back to evidence-quality-only ranking and note this. This keeps the pipeline reproducible while documenting external data as a fetched input.\n\nScoring for \"high-confidence mosaic nonsense\":\n- Candidates with stop_gained annotation.\n- Hard requirements: alt_reads >= 4, both strands, AF in [0.02, 0.50], depth >= 20, mean_alt_baseq >= 25, end fraction <= 0.5.\n- Rank: gene constraint (LOEUF ascending, pLI descending) as primary key; then depth; then AF closeness to mosaic range.\nPick top → STXBP1.\n\nAllele fraction reporting: 18/93 = 0.19354... Report rounded to 0.1935 (0–1 scale).\n\nvariant.tsv columns: chrom,pos,ref,alt,gene,consequence,alt_reads,total_reads,allele_fraction → chr9 127661125 G T STXBP1 stop_gained 18 93 0.1935\n\nConsequence field: \"stop_gained\" (SO). Might include HGVS in evidence/report.\n\nEvidence.json contents:\n- Task, timestamp, reference genome (GRCh38, GATK bundle, chr9, length), annotation (GENCODE v47), aligner minimap2 2.31-r1302 -x sr, filters (mapq/bq),\n- Read stats: total FASTQ reads 431396, aligned primary 427460, used mapq>=20: 417753, covered positions.\n- Selected variant details: position, ref/alt, depth, alt fwd/rev, AF, mean alt BQ, ends=0, codon GAA>TAA, p.Glu117*, transcript list (MANE/canonical representative ENST00000373302.8; also c.307/p.Glu103* for shorter transcripts), strand +, gene ID.\n- Constraint evidence: gnomAD GRCh38 API values (LOEUF 0.0987, pLI 1.0, obs/exp lof 3/78.5, lof_z 7.23), OR1J1 no_exp_lof.\n- Excluded candidate OR1J1 with reasons.\n- Validation: independent recount numbers.\n\nreport.md: full narrative with methods, versions, results, evidence, limitations.\n\nOK — one more validation: independent recount + sequence context. Then update analysis.py (frame check fix + final output generation), re-run to produce the outputs. Then verify the outputs.\n\nOne more check — is there a known dbSNP ID for chr9:127661125 G>T? Let me quickly try NCBI Variation API:\nhttps://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/variation/v0/spdi/chr9:127661125:G:T/rs… hmm, the endpoint is: GET https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/variation/v0/spdi/{seq}/{pos}/{del}/{ins} returns RS ID if present? Actually, the SPDI service canonicalizes; there's also a dbSNP search. Let me try: https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/variation/v0/spdi/NC_000009.12:127661125:G:T/seq_id… I think the format is /spdi/{sequence}:{position}:{deleted}:{inserted}/canonical_representative or /rs. Let me just try and see. If there's an rs ID, it might be a known germline variant (bad for mosaic) — worth knowing. AF 0.194 strongly suggests mosaic anyway, but the check adds robustness.\n"}}
